  "course": {
    "narrative": "A 69-year-old African American gentleman with stage IV chronic kidney disease secondary to polycystic kidney disease returned for followup. He reported feeling well and denied any shortness of breath, chest pain, swelling, nausea, or vomiting. His blood pressure control significantly improved after prior counseling on medication adherence, and his home blood pressure readings were also better. Physical examination showed a blood pressure of 140/57 and pulse of 58 with no significant edema. Laboratory results showed a stable creatinine of 4.5, ionized calcium of 8.5, intact PTH of 458, and stable hemoglobin of 10.9. No medication changes were made. He was re-instructed on attending dialysis and transplant education classes and needs urology follow-up for elevated PSA. He is scheduled to return in 4 to 6 weeks.",
